  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another round of heavy rain is on the horizon \nSaturday into Saturday night, which could bring the next round \nof flash flooding to the region. Unsettled weather continues\nthrough Sunday. \n\nToday will be pleasant with a cold front moving offshore with\nour region drying out. We will get a brief reprieve from the\nmuggy conditions and wet weather with a rather pleasant day\nexpected. Highs get into the upper 70s to low 80s with mostly\nsunny to partly cloudy skies.\n\nOur attention now turns to Saturday afternoon and evening. An\narea of low pressure will move in from the southwest bringing\nanother round of heavy rain to the region. PWATs are again\nexpected to get near 2\" mark with model soundings showing a tall\nand skinny CAPE profile. Warm cloud layers also will be over 10k\nfeet AGL, indicating that any storms that move through will be\nquite the efficient rainfall producers. We are starting to get\ninto range of the CAMs as well. Guidance is all over the place\nregarding the placement of the axis of heaviest rainfall, but\nsome of the QPF numbers are a bit concerning. Both the NAM and\nthe HRRR have bullseyes over 4\" for different parts of the area.\nAdditonally, most areas yesterday saw over an inch of rain, \nwith the highest amounts of 4 to 6\". So, we are quite \nsaturated, making it easier to flood in most spots. Will have to\nsee if guidance later today starts to hone in on where the \nheaviest rain will fall before considering any Flood Watches. \nHowever, WPC has added a Slight (2/4) Risk for Excessive \nRainfall for most of area - south of I-78. The risk is certainly\nthere for more flash flooding. \n\nIn terms of the severe weather risk, parameters (shear,\ninstability, moisture, forcing) are all lower compared to what\nwe saw yesterday. While a strong to severe thunderstorm can't be\nruled out completely, the threat for damaging\nwinds/tornadoes/hail is much lower compared to what we saw\nThursday. \n\nSome showers could linger through Sunday on the backside of the\nlow but not expecting anything significant through the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Fair weather is expected to return for early \nnext week.\n\nMore zonal flow with some subtle ridging moves in for the first\nhalf of the week. At the surface, high pressure will mainly be\nin control, resulting in dry and tranquil weather through\nWednesday. Temperatures should be seasonable through this\nstretch. Another frontal system looks to move in towards\nThursday-Friday, but too early to speculate on specific impacts.",
